WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Narrator: This is one of the
biggest stunts in "Fast 9,"

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.070
the latest "Fast &amp; Furious" movie.

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.360
It's a four-second shot

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.920
they could have made entirely out of CGI,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.098
but instead, they spent eight months

00:00:12.098 --> 00:00:14.530
planning the stunt with real vehicles.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.240
The "Fast" crew broke down
the shot into two parts

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.410
and built this track to carry
the car through the window

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.480
and into the back of this truck.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.300
Then they programmed two
motion-controlled cameras

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.690
to perfectly sync,

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:30.130
and then stitched it all
into one continuous shot.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.160
This was just one of many
over-the-top stunts in "F9,"

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.330
which were all done with
as little CGI as possible.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.745
Justin Lin: We see cars every day.

00:00:37.745 --> 00:00:40.320
So anytime you go to kind of a CG option,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.060
if you're just off by a tiny bit,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.740
the brain knows it's not real.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.847
Narrator: And spoilers ahead for "F9."

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:52.680
Car flips are a common stunt
in the "Fast" franchise,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.679
but for "F9," the production team needed

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:56.477
to take this move a step further

00:00:56.477 --> 00:01:00.812
when Letty uses a magnetized
car to latch onto an SUV

00:01:00.812 --> 00:01:04.200
during the film's climactic
chase in Tbilisi, Georgia.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.320
Yeah, that's right.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.720
That's a car that attaches to another car

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.360
and then flips, without CGI.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.630
To get this to work,

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:14.010
the team had to reconfigure Letty's car

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:15.700
so it could tow the second vehicle

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.530
and then release it at the right moment.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.990
First, stunt coordinator
Andy Gill and his team

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:22.090
removed the trunk.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.360
Then they replaced it with a steel cage

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.060
so the SUV's wheel would lock into place.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.660
To make it look like the SUV

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.551
is being sucked into
the trunk by a magnet,

00:01:31.551 --> 00:01:33.670
the crew built a ramp behind Letty's car

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.500
so the SUV could easily hop onto it.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.290
A weighted overhead cable system

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.460
then helped with the final backflip.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.880
When the SUV hit the end of the line,

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.423
the cables yanked it backwards.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.057
Now that the crew could attach and flip

00:01:50.057 --> 00:01:53.020
one car from a moving
vehicle, how about two?

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.680
That's what happens
when Dom pulls two SUVs

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.610
to his magnetized car
before repelling them.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.685
One SUV survives its jump.

00:02:00.685 --> 00:02:02.543
The other SUV leaps in the air

00:02:02.543 --> 00:02:05.650
thanks to a hidden ramp
built by the stunt team.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.460
And that SUV is destroyed
when it hits this small car

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.680
followed by this parked van.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.050
Andy Gill: And then
that car was pinned down

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.240
on a big steel plate in the ground,

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.490
so it wasn't going to move.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.560
Narrator: To stop the SUV's momentum,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.700
the van also needed a wall inside.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.660
Gill: I just had effects
build a steel wall.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.110
If here's your car,

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.310
right here is the firewall
all the way up to the roof,

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.970
and it put kickers back so
you can't get through it.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.960
Then it blows through that second car

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.870
and gets halfway through
and hits that steel plate.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.370
And that's what turns it over.

00:02:42.345 --> 00:02:44.600
Narrator: Cables just weren't
enough for this stunt,

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.920
when a car is flipped over on its side.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.310
While this elaborate flip
could have been completely CG,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.810
Gill found a way to do it practically.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.430
The key for this scene was
something called a flipper.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.260
Basically, the car's placed on a giant pad

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.650
with two hydraulic arms that extend

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:02.760
and cause the pad to flip up,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.910
launching the car along with it.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:06.490
And that henchman you see here

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.050
didn't actually get hit by this car.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.510
To flip the car without hurting anyone,

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.480
they shot the scene twice.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.430
The first shot had the
performer standing on the car,

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.130
but there was no flip.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.570
In the second, they flipped
the car from the same position,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.660
but with no actor.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.020
These two shots were then merged together

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.633
in postproduction.

00:03:30.841 --> 00:03:34.160
Magnet-charged stunts didn't
just involve other cars.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.930
For this moment, it was all
about kitchen appliances.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.690
Unlike the flipper move,

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.620
it was possible to have
extras in this shot,

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.450
but that added another risk factor.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:46.808
Gill: Yeah, you don't want
to be in front of one,

00:03:46.808 --> 00:03:48.360
I can tell you that. [chuckles]

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.600
Narrator: Before placing any extras,

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.693
Gill used sandbags to see
how safe each mark was.

00:03:54.693 --> 00:03:57.340
And location was everything.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.570
We put a lot of time and effort

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.220
into picking the right store for that.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.460
Had big windows, the
sidewalk was just right,

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:06.210
we didn't have trees or
telephone poles in the way

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.240
for them to come out.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.540
Narrator: Launching the appliances

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.870
involved first welding
a tube into each one.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:15.180
That tube was then
inserted into another tube

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.960
filled with compressed air.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.770
Once that air was released,

00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:22.040
the appliance would shoot
out like a cannonball.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.680
Gill: So, as the car's coming up,

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.504
I would say, "Three, two, one, now,"

00:04:26.504 --> 00:04:27.704
and we'd videotape it all,

00:04:27.704 --> 00:04:29.270
and I'd put white marks on the ground

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:32.330
so I'd know exactly when I
said "one" where the car was

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.040
and then where those
machines were coming out.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.780
By doing all the tests,

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.540
we knew that there was a big
brick wall between windows.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:40.373
It was going to be tight,

00:04:40.373 --> 00:04:41.660
it was going to be a
lot of stuff coming out,

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:42.810
broken glass and all that.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:43.890
But they could hunker down,

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.980
and people in the shot just looks good.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.180
Narrator: Dodging
washing machines is hard,

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.053
but what about explosives?

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:55.720
During the first big chase of "F9,"

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.010
Dom and his brother, Jake,

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:58.410
try to outpace each other

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.100
on jungle roads filled with land mines.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.760
Filming on location with real explosives

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:05.640
meant dealing with unpredictable weather

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.240
and smoke-induced visibility issues.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.180
Instead of using the
overgrown muddy jungle roads

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.940
on location in Thailand,

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.440
the crew built their own roads
out of decomposed granite,

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.970
a much more suitable material for racing.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.611
In the end, they only created

00:05:20.611 --> 00:05:22.661
about a half mile of new roads,

00:05:22.661 --> 00:05:25.260
but the camera crew, using helicopters,

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.250
drones, and off-road buggies,

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.020
could make it feel much bigger on camera.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.130
Gill: So it's short sections
that we'd use over and over,

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.070
and we would film in different directions,

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:34.960
so it looks different.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:36.160
Narrator: The crew had to consider

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:39.420
even the tiniest details
to keep the drivers safe.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.560
To make sure fires didn't
start near any of the drivers,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:43.930
the crew wet down the mulch

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:46.370
that flew up in the air with each boom,

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:47.910
and they welded steel plates

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.770
under the driver and
passenger sides of each car

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:53.100
to provide an extra layer of protection

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.530
between the passengers and
any of the 30 real mortars

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:58.030
planted in the road.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.290
The stunt drivers also
limited their speeds

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:02.500
to below 50 miles per hour,

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:03.380
even though in the movie

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:05.540
they're supposed to be going at least 80.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:06.670
Gill: A lot of times,

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.840
we're not going as fast as
you would think in a chase,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.090
but it looks a lot faster

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:13.870
'cause we're usually in confined areas.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.340
Where you really want to speed it up

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:18.000
is when you have big, wide-open areas

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.230
and there's not really anything
relative to your speed.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.310
So you could be doing 120,
and it doesn't look fast.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.710
Narrator: To ensure that
the main cars looked fast,

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.900
other cars had to slow down.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.460
Gill: We'll do wipes with cars

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:32.100
that are only going 10 miles an hour,

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.765
so you're going by them really fast.

00:06:33.765 --> 00:06:35.750
Narrator: And the climax of this sequence

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:37.713
is even more extreme.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:42.930
This is a moment when Dom and Letty

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.010
jump from one cliff to another,

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.390
attached to land by just one rope.

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:49.960
Even for a "Fast" movie, this was risky.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:52.190
But the crew managed to
create the scene practically

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.690
with just a little help from CGI.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:58.230
They filmed in a quarry with
a 60- to 70-foot drop-off

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:00.170
to imitate the cliff's edge.

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:02.460
On that edge, they reconstructed the end

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.370
of the rickety wooden bridge
as well as a steep ramp.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.450
So the car really did jump.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.670
Gill: We could jump, hit that post,

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:13.220
and then land still on the road.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:15.550
Narrator: Careful camera
placement obscuring the quarry

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:18.560
made it look much higher on screen.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.610
The swing was CGI, but the
swing and eventual landing

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.630
still needed to try to
follow the rules of physics.

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:28.240
So Gill first conducted a toy-car test

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:29.610
for the swing and landing,

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.710
like he did with many stunts in the film.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.830
Here, he tied one to a string
and ran it over a table.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.480
Gill: The toy car came off like that.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.810
It fell really nicely for a while.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.270
And as soon as that caught, what happened,

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.160
instead of just swinging like this,

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:48.930
it snapped it around almost
upside down this way,

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:50.710
and then back and around.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:51.850
Narrator: For the landing,

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.760
they used a real car
rigged to complex cables

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:58.700
strategically placed on the
hood and around the back wheels.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:00.830
As the car got closer to its mark,

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:02.640
another cable attached to the nose

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.490
stopped the front of the
car from going any further.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:08.590
The forward momentum then
swung the back around,

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:10.771
hitting its mark perfectly.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.040
All of these stunts were extreme,

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:18.310
but nothing compared
to the extensive chase

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:20.240
involving what may be the franchise's

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:23.200
most ambitious custom build yet.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:24.630
This is the Armadillo,

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.421
a 14-foot-high, 26-ton,

00:08:27.421 --> 00:08:30.037
three-section armored monster.

00:08:30.037 --> 00:08:32.990
This beast of a vehicle had
to navigate narrow streets

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.620
while doing everything
from driving on its back

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:37.540
to sliding down a huge cliff.

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:39.840
Between this and all the magnet stunts,

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.120
it's no wonder the entire Tbilisi chase

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.700
took nearly a year to bring to life.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.460
Four of those months were
dedicated to custom-building

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.410
two versions of the Armadillo,

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.650
one for driving it like a normal truck,

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:55.080
another for making it drivable
when it's turned upside down.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:59.120
Those trucks are really,
really heavy, as we found out.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:00.940
Narrator: Instead of building new roads,

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:02.710
they worked with what was already there,

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.810
first by carefully
selecting the run-up area.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:07.210
These are the portions of road

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:09.500
where the cars in a
sequence can gather speed

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:12.190
before getting to the piece
of road they're filming on.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:13.820
Because it was so massive,

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:17.660
the Armadillo needed a lot
of room to get up to speed,

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:20.210
and it also needed a run-out area,

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.400
or extra road after the shot ends

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:26.030
so nobody has to slam their
brakes and kill the momentum.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:28.910
Even though the Armadillo was fast enough,

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:31.350
Gill found that getting
right up close to it

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.770
could make it look even faster.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.570
Because the Armadillo
is so heavily armored,

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.580
the crew used all the tools they had

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.900
in new and imaginative ways.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:44.070
For example, they could make
a dent thanks to the flipper,

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:47.450
which was just off camera
launching cars over here.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:50.083
And the parked cars
seen at these two angles

00:09:50.083 --> 00:09:51.808
were attached to cables on the ground

00:09:51.808 --> 00:09:53.216
that, when cut loose,

00:09:53.216 --> 00:09:56.620
could be pulled right
into the Armadillo's path.

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:59.310
Because so many parts of
these impressive feats

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:01.757
were done in-camera, every little detail

00:10:01.757 --> 00:10:04.070
needed to be laid out far in advance.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:05.890
But it's the experimentation

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:07.980
and commitment to practical effects

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:11.327
that make every flip and
crash feel so visceral.

00:10:11.327 --> 00:10:13.908
That's what has always
set this franchise apart

00:10:13.908 --> 00:10:15.949
from its humble beginnings.

00:10:15.949 --> 00:10:18.130
Crew: Good job, man. Nice job.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:20.210
I think that when you
do things practically,

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:24.340
when two elements interact,
it almost defies physics

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.610
because you can't
mathematically predict it

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:27.980
because there's so many variables.

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:29.420
And there's something
very visceral about that

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:30.690
that we connect to.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.300
I have my toy cars all the time,

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.150
and Justin's eyes light up
every time I walk in with them

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:38.000
because he knows we're going to play.

